Z||000016||lou_dias||06-29-2001||09:58 AM||||Actually, the opposite.<BR>I'm just speaking from personal experience.<BR>I had this ex that hadn't gotten it in 4 years.  It took 3.5 months before she "decided" to "allow" me to have sex with her.  She has a depression disorder and was supposed to be on medication for it and I know that affected things but it set the tone for when and how often we would do it.  A relationship involves catering to each other's needs.  She didn't cater to mine too often and we went out for over 1.5 years.  Maybe 3 times a month didn't cut the mustard for me.<BR>  Then I met this girl 2 years ago.  I fell in love with her and she taught I was a great guy/friend.  I comforted her after her boyfriend dumped her.  And remained just a friend until I felt she was over him.  She knew I was interested in her the whole time.  So her excuse is that it would ruin our friendship.  How lame.  Anyway, as a guy, if you want someone and they just want to be friends.  It sucks to be around them all the time.  So I pushed her away and said see ya.  We started talking again 6 months ago and even went out on a couple of dates.  Made out with her.  But then things stopped.  I think she just let it go that far to say "see, I don't feel it so lets be friends, ok?"  I didn't buy it and said adios again.  I truly believe she "loves" me as a person but I have enough friends that I don't spend time with as it is.<BR>Recently, I have been involved with a girl who I never planned on getting involved with.  We became best friends and have been sleeping together since just before that.  We would tell everyone we were just friends, now we spend so much time together that we admit to oursleves and anyone who asks that we are "dating".  We're both unsure about commitment because of how somethings just change but we spend too much time together to know that there is no one else.  I'm very happy.  It gets better because she used to be a "dancer" and just the other day she ran into an ex co-worker who was very friendly and she asked me if I'd want a 3-some with this new girl.  It's reasons like this that we are still "officially" uncommitted.  I would never do that with a "serious girlfriend" but since we are best friends, I might as well take advantage of the benefits.<P>Besides, wouldn't the whole boat on the water with the fireworks going and some champagne make for the perfect "romantic" setting to consumate a relationship?||||reg||1
